  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "The key of a text of a set is its full name: MyApp.Translations.ShopTranslations.Checkout for ShopTranslations.Checkout in the namespace MyApp.Translations, where it was Shop.Checkout. The old key left out the namespace and a trailing Translations, so the texts of two sets of one name shared a key: sets in two namespaces, in a library and in the app that uses it, or ShopTranslationsEN next to ShopEN. Now each text has a key of its own. A set in the global namespace gives ShopTranslations.Checkout, and a namespace part named like a keyword comes without its @.\n\nThe package itself never reads the key, so every text shows the wording it showed. Only an app or library that looks wording up by the key, as in a resource file through IStringLocalizer, has something to do: rename each entry to the full name of its text, which is the name your code calls the text by, with its namespace in front.\n\nA language code that is wrong is not a culture name in the messages of the build and of the registry, where it was no culture name (LOC010, LOC018), and LOC016 names a brace that is not a placeholder. Only a test that holds such a message to its wording needs the new one.\n\nThe details have a page per subject under docs/, which the README links to: how Idioma works, language codes, libraries that use Idioma, Blazor, what the build rejects, and coming from resx. New in them: your users get the languages of the app's own project, so an app that keeps its texts in a library declares its languages in both; what the build misses in a call of Format, where a value too few throws when the call runs; and the class the source generator writes to register your sets, which is public in an assembly that has sets.\n\nThe demos are SetDemo.cs and InlineTextDemo.cs, and run in a codespace from the README.",
